**Severity:** LOW · **CWE-327**

The convergent dedup envelope added in crates/bud-codec/src/shared_context.rs bases confidentiality on `derive_tag`, a hand-written unkeyed add/multiply/rotate mix seeded with the SHA-256 IV and used as hash, KDF, PRF and keystream at once. In `put` (lines 453-463) the content key is `derive_tag("convergent-key/v1", content_digest)` and that same digest is the envelope id the function returns, so the identifier is the key's preimage: anybody who holds or observes an envelope id recomputes the key and decrypts the record without any owner secret, making the `OwnerKeyWrap` entries decorative. The authentication tag is `derive_tag("convergent-auth/v1", ciphertext)`, unkeyed, so any party recomputes it over modified ciphertext and the check in `open` (line 520) passes, and the wrap mask depends only on the owner secret, so two wraps of the same owner cancel. All arithmetic was confirmed against a faithful u64 replica of the exact Rust loops: given only the id, the stored ciphertext decrypts to the plaintext. The module is declared `WIRING: unwired` with no caller anywhere in the tree, so no deployed path is reachable today.

