**TL;DR:** `AptosUpstream::url_for` in `src/rpc/compat/aptos.rs` concatenates the caller's path onto the upstream base and re-parses it, so `/v1/accounts/..\..\metrics` passes the route table but is forwarded to `https://node/metrics`.

**Impact:** Any unauthenticated client of the public Aptos endpoint can drive the operator's internal full node to serve arbitrary GET/POST paths outside `/v1`, bypassing the forwarding allowlist and returning those responses to the caller.

**Root cause:**
- The forwarder builds the upstream request by string-concatenating the caller path onto the base and re-parsing it, so URL normalisation happens after the route allowlist and rewrites the path that was approved.
- `classify()`/`segments()` split the path on `/` only, while the WHATWG parser used by `reqwest::Url::parse` also treats a backslash as a path separator and collapses `.`/`..` segments, so `..\..\metrics` is accepted as a single placeholder value and then normalises away.
- Nothing after the parse checks that the resulting URL is still on the base origin and inside the `/v1` prefix the upstream was configured with.

```suggestion
    fn url_for(&self, uri: &hyper::Uri) -> Result<reqwest::Url, Box<HttpResponse>> {
        let suffix = uri
            .path_and_query()
            .map(|value| value.as_str())
            .unwrap_or("/v1");
        // `base` already ends in `/v1`; append only the route-relative part.
        // Concatenating the complete downstream URI would duplicate the version prefix.
        let relative = suffix.strip_prefix("/v1").unwrap_or(suffix);
        // The parser treats a backslash as a separator and collapses `.`/`..`
        // segments after concatenation, so a route-shaped path can still
        // normalise out of the configured `/v1` prefix.
        let dot_segment = relative
            .split('?')
            .next()
            .unwrap_or(relative)
            .split(|character| character == '/' || character == '\\')
            .any(|segment| {
                segment.eq_ignore_ascii_case(".")
                    || segment.eq_ignore_ascii_case("..")
                    || segment.eq_ignore_ascii_case("%2e")
                    || segment.eq_ignore_ascii_case("%2e%2e")
                    || segment.eq_ignore_ascii_case("..%2e")
                    || segment.eq_ignore_ascii_case("%2e.")
            });
        let target = format!("{}{relative}", self.base.as_str().trim_end_matches('/'));
        let url = reqwest::Url::parse(&target).map_err(|_| {
            Box::new(proxy_error(
                StatusCode::BAD_REQUEST,
                "invalid_aptos_path",
                "The Aptos request path is not a valid upstream URL",
            ))
        })?;
        let inside_prefix = url.origin() == self.base.origin()
            && (url.path() == "/v1" || url.path().starts_with("/v1/"));
        if dot_segment || !inside_prefix {
            return Err(Box::new(proxy_error(
                StatusCode::BAD_REQUEST,
                "invalid_aptos_path",
                "The Aptos request path leaves the configured upstream prefix",
            )));
        }
        Ok(url)
    }
```

