### 🟡 Unbounded frame-declared symbol count lets a 27-byte B3F1 frame force a 1.3 MB decoder allocation, once per attacker-chosen segment

**Severity:** MEDIUM

**TL;DR:** decode_wire bounds source_symbols only by self-consistency, so a 27-byte frame makes extract_object_from_frames allocate ~1.3 MB per distinct segment; the meter is charged for payload bytes only.

**Impact:** Whoever can hand frames to the B3F1 decoder (the screen/stream ingress this module documents) spends 27 bytes per 1.3 MB allocation and a few kilobytes of frames to exhaust gigabytes of the decoding node or client. No in-tree caller wires that ingress yet.

**Root cause:**
- The frame parser treats its own header as the authority on coding geometry: source_symbols sizes the allocation and is also the value the segment-length check is compared against, so nothing ever compares it with the registered profile ceiling.
- decode_wire accepts any source_symbols that merely divides segment_len; neither field is ever bounded by limits.rs k_max or RFC6330_MAX_SOURCE_SYMBOLS, and the only charge is for the payload the frame actually carried.
- two_path.rs derives the ephemeral Profile from the frame (k_max = frame.source_symbols), which makes SegmentDecoder::new's `len > profile.segment_bytes()` check vacuous, inserts one decoder per attacker-chosen segment with no cap, and leaves raptorq's `vec![None; source_symbols]` (24 bytes per symbol) unpaid by the meter.

