### 🔵 Custody quota and stored-bytes accounting ignore the peer-supplied placement vector in `ShareStore::accept`

**Severity:** LOW

**TL;DR:** `ShareStore::accept` in `mobile/core/src/store.rs` meters a share by `symbol.len()` only, then retains the peer-supplied `placed` vector in full, so an assignment can hold unbounded memory the quota never counts.

**Impact:** A peer that hands out share assignments can hold memory far beyond the quota the user set while the status panel reports almost nothing stored; no assignment transport is wired in this branch, so it is not exploitable in this build.

**Root cause:**
- The store's only bound on custody data is `ShareRecord::size()`, which was defined as the symbol length alone even though every retained share also owns a `placed` placement vector that is supplied by the assigning peer.
- `accept()` meters a share with `assigned.symbol.len()` and then moves `assigned.placed` into the retained record; the only placement checks are ESI ascending order and `placed[position] == (esi, leaf)`, both satisfiable for any vector length, so nothing bounds `placed.len()`.
- Every consumer of `size()` inherits the under-count — `stored_bytes()`, the quota check, `largest_id`/`release_until_quota`, the free-space floor in `MobileCore::accept_share`, and `NodeStatus.stored_bytes` in the FFI — so the operator-facing figure reports ~0 bytes for the retained placement.

```suggestion
    pub fn size(&self) -> u64 {
        let symbol = self.symbol.len() as u64;
        let placed = (self.placed.len() as u64).saturating_mul(36);
        symbol.saturating_add(placed)
    }
```

